  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1040 PM MDT Mon Aug 17 2026\n\nHigh pressure is building into the southwest, with a ridge \ndeveloping across the Great Basin. Flow aloft has turned zonal, and \nthe expected moisture increase is evident in the form of increased \ncloud cover across northern Utah and Wyoming. A weak frontal \nboundary is accompanying this moisture, and will help to bring \nincreased cloud cover and shower activity to northeast Utah and \nnorthwest Colorado, as well as along portions of the northern and \ncentral Divide Tuesday. This boundary will track southeast into \nWednesday, bringing more activity to portions of the central and \nsouthern Divide. Dry surface levels and relatively modest PWATs (on \nthe order of 100-140% of normal) will limit heavy rain potential the \nnext few days. The most likely impacts from any showers or \nthunderstorms will be gusty, erratic outflow winds and frequent \nlightning. Outside of shower activity, look from some breezy \nafternoon winds, with gusts of 20-25 mph. Temperatures will run 5-10 \ndegrees above normal.\n\nHigh pressure remains centered around the Four Corners through the \nend of the week, keeping temperatures above normal and generally dry \nconditions. However, a pattern shift is looking to set up late week \nand into the weekend. A trough moves toward the West Coast late \nweek, nudging the center of the high eastward. There's a signal for \na return to monsoonal flow, as moisture-rich southerly to \nsouthwesterly flow sets up over the Desert Southwest. However, there \nremains quite a bit of spread over how much moisture will reach \neastern Utah and western Colorado. This should become clearer with \nsubsequent model runs. Increasing moisture will bring increased \ncloud cover and more showers and thunderstorms for the coming \nweekend, along with temperatures dropping back to near-normal values.",
